From d638469c63201f26a9c2579f861680a84543a28c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:41:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] soc: apple: skip unreadable AOP scan pages and capture
 early

The device E1 scan stopped at 0x8035f0000 after 56557568 readable
bytes, leaving most RAM unexamined. Confine every E1 copy to one
physical page, quarantine failures and continue. Explicitly exclude
memblock-reserved, PageReserved, no-map and non-System RAM pages.
Drop an unreadable watch hit without stopping the other candidates;
keep the separate reserved-ring IIO fault latch unchanged.

Track disjoint readable/skipped PFNs, per-reason byte totals and
unvisited RAM. Coalesce skipped ranges with a fixed storage cap and
disclose omitted range bytes without losing coverage or quarantine.
Count skipped pages toward pacing and the existing traversal/deadline
limits. Never retry a quarantined PFN, including cross-page probes.

Add immutable ring_scan_early=1 with debug=1 ring_scan=1. Capture at
postcore_initcall_sync before platform population and most device
allocations, then expose the frozen result through the observer.
Report early failure instead of silently substituting a late scan.
This cannot undo initial allocations or RAM reuse by a prior kernel.

The host ASan/UBSan harness compiles the production E1 loop/copy
functions, injects the reported fault and verifies a later ring hit,
quarantine, coverage revocation, skip coalescing/truncation, pacing
and byte/time/hit/signal bounds. No device access was performed.

Signed-off-by: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
